<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yevgenia Ginzburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Eugenia Ginzburg, Yevgenia Solomonovna Ginzburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yevgenia Ginzburg was a Soviet writer and Communist Party educator who served an 18-year sentence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolyma">Kolyma</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> and told of it in her memoir Journey into the Whirlwind. A docent in the history of the party at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and wife of the city's mayor, Pavel Aksyonov, she was accused after the assassination of Sergei <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirov">Kirov</a> of belonging to a counter-revolutionary Trotskyist group, expelled from the party and arrested in February 1937. At a trial she later said took seven minutes, she was sentenced to ten years. Released in 1949 but kept in exile, she was rehabilitated in 1955. Her memoir, unpublishable in the USSR, was smuggled abroad and published in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yevgenia-ginzburg.jpg" alt="Yevgenia Ginzburg" width="330" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yevgenia_Ginzburg_Kazan_Mid_1920s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
